TASK-260715-10phgg,task,run-macos-full-degraded-recovery-validation,"Verify Mac full, degraded, failed, and relay-restoration behavior",backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Run the M2 capability acceptance matrix on a named physical Mac, proving truthful modes, TCP continuity where safe, tunnel-owned DNS fallback, explicit UDP rejection, mandatory failure on safe-DNS loss, clean relay restoration, no ordinary leak, and bounded resources.","In scope: supported physical Apple-silicon Mac and macOS; stable Ethernet or Wi-Fi starting path; controlled authenticated SSH host; supported and unsupported relay fixture; full startup; install or handshake failure; relay process or lane loss; controlled TCP flow; general UDP probes; UDP and TCP DNS clients; safe-DNS failure; reprobe recovery; provider stop; physical and exit captures; snapshots; memory and counters; redacted evidence. Out of scope: path transitions, sleep or wake, NAT64 transition, captive recovery, includeAllNetworks, final QUIC policy, notarization, production traffic, legacy app behavior, and unsupported Intel client hardware.","1. Evidence records Mac, macOS and Xcode, source and dependency revisions, profile generation, relay asset and protocol identity, reason fixture, resolver and exit fixture, network type, timestamps, duration, snapshots, memory, queues, associations, drops, and authorized captures. 2. Full startup advertises UDP only after relay validation; forced relay-unavailable startup advertises degraded only after controlled TCP and safe DNS work. 3. Runtime relay loss invalidates general UDP, keeps eligible TCP and safe DNS, reports the exact reason, and physical capture shows zero ordinary UDP or DNS fallback; safe-DNS loss moves to failed. 4. Relay repair and reprobe restore full only after a fresh identity and handshake, and subsequent IPv4 or IPv6 UDP succeeds through new exit-host associations without old-generation delivery. 5. Repeated full, degraded, failed, restore, and stop rows stay within the memory budget, clean routes and resources, and attach a TASK-ID-scoped redacted reproducible evidence bundle.","[""TASK-260715-2y78ah"",""TASK-260715-2m7lwo""]","[""TASK-260715-3kga9i""]","[{""text"":""Run full startup degraded startup runtime failure DNS failure restore and stop on Mac"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Capture truthful snapshots traffic behavior and zero ordinary physical fallback"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Attach device revision memory generation cleanup and redacted evidence"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:51:42Z,2026-07-15T02:04:35Z,true,[],"[{""name"":""TASK-260715-10phgg_m1-macos-baseline.md"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-10phgg/TASK-260715-10phgg_m1-macos-baseline.md"",""description"":""Physical Mac M1 acceptance prerequisite for capability validation""}]",[]
TASK-260715-1vg1mb,task,add-capability-state-and-fault-injection-tests,Add capability state-machine and fault-injection tests,backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Build deterministic tests for every full, degraded, failed, stop, and relay-reprobe state and event, including readiness combinations, finite reasons, generation ordering, association invalidation, safe-DNS loss, cancellation, memory overlap, and snapshot compatibility.","In scope: table-driven state transitions; fake base TCP, safe DNS, bootstrap, relay, association, health, clock, jitter, memory, and snapshot dependencies; every reason code; startup full and degraded; runtime loss; simultaneous failures; safe-DNS mandatory failure; restore; stop; duplicate and late events; provider-message versions; counters and cleanup. Out of scope: real sockets, remote hosts, physical routing captures, M3 path reconnect, final UI snapshot tests, protocol byte fuzzing, and performance tuning.","1. The transition table executes every legal state and event pair and rejects impossible or stale pairs without false-full, double cleanup, or hidden fallthrough. 2. All finite bootstrap, protocol, process, lane, association, safe-DNS, memory, stop, and retry reasons produce the contractually correct mode, capability bits, association action, and retry eligibility. 3. Simultaneous relay and DNS loss, stop during bootstrap or reprobe, duplicate terminal events, old-generation callbacks, and snapshot requests at each boundary are deterministic. 4. Fake-clock and memory fixtures prove one timer and attempt, capped backoff, overlap ceiling, stable reset, cancellation, and no task, buffer, channel, association, timer, or snapshot growth over repeated cycles. 5. Golden snapshots verify schema versions, system-session separation, redaction, unknown-future handling, aggregate diagnostics, and no destinations, queries, payloads, credentials, or remote text.","[""TASK-260715-3edgwz"",""TASK-260715-ak0s72"",""TASK-260715-3260rm"",""TASK-260715-uh8kk6"",""TASK-260715-kxxujt""]","[""TASK-260715-2y78ah""]","[{""text"":""Execute every state event reason and readiness combination deterministically"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Stress simultaneous stop stale-generation retry and memory-overlap races"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Verify versioned redacted snapshots and all resource baselines"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T01:58:45Z,true,[],[],[]
TASK-260715-2y78ah,task,add-degraded-routing-dns-leak-integration-tests,"Add degraded routing, DNS leak, and recovery integration tests",backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Exercise the composed M2 runtime in controlled harness and macOS development contexts across startup full or degraded, relay failures, UDP rejection, TCP continuity, safe DNS, mandatory DNS loss, reprobe restoration, and physical-path sentinels.","In scope: composed M1 packet, TCP, DNS, SSH, and provider seams; M2 bootstrap and relay fixtures; controlled TCP, UDP, and DNS endpoints; access-side physical DNS and UDP sentinels; full startup; unsupported asset; checksum or version failure; process and lane failure; general UDP rejection; safe DNS-over-TCP or approved DoH; TCP continuity; DNS loss; recovery; stop; counters; repeated runs; redaction. Out of scope: physical iPhone acceptance, path switching, NAT64 transition, captive portal, includeAllNetworks, lane pool scheduling, final QUIC policy, and public resolver benchmarking.","1. Full startup carries TCP, general UDP, and DNS through the intended SSH paths, while startup relay failure publishes degraded only after TCP and safe DNS are usable. 2. Every full-to-degraded trigger stops and invalidates UDP before the snapshot, preserves controlled TCP according to M1 policy, and keeps client UDP and TCP DNS on the approved tunneled fallback. 3. Physical DNS and UDP sentinels observe zero ordinary fallback during connected, transition, degraded, failed, reprobe, and stop fault rows; safe-DNS loss produces mandatory failure. 4. Reprobe restores full only after a fresh validated relay generation and subsequent UDP and DNS traffic uses new associations with no late old-generation delivery. 5. Repeated scenarios reconcile system session, capability snapshots, TCP flows, DNS transactions, relay generation, associations, queues, channels, timers, routes, drops, and memory and return resources to baseline.","[""TASK-260715-1vg1mb"",""TASK-260715-1ut6ot""]","[""TASK-260715-3nkhry"",""TASK-260715-10phgg"",""TASK-260715-3kga9i""]","[{""text"":""Exercise full degraded failed and restored traffic behavior in the composed harness"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Prove zero ordinary physical DNS or UDP fallback across every fault row"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Reconcile snapshots generations traffic counters memory and cleanup repeatedly"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T02:04:35Z,true,[],"[{""name"":""TASK-260715-2y78ah_m1-harness-handoff.md"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-2y78ah/TASK-260715-2y78ah_m1-harness-handoff.md"",""description"":""M1 composed routing and DNS harness prerequisite""}]",[]
TASK-260715-2zmw58,task,record-degraded-safe-dns-fallback-policy,Record the degraded safe-DNS fallback policy,backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Translate the approved M1 exit-resolver decision into an exact M2 degraded-mode upstream selection and health contract, using the existing SSH DNS-over-TCP implementation by default or a separately approved tunneled DoH path without physical fallback.","In scope: consume TASK-260715-1tnjlu and resulting resolver schema; DNS-over-TCP direct-tcpip baseline; conditions for configured tunneled DoH if the approved policy includes it; resolver endpoint and identity; address-family order; transport priority; timeout and retry; connection pooling input; TC behavior; cache generation; health readiness; fail-safe outcome; profile migration; test fixtures; privacy and UI implications. Out of scope: selecting an unapproved public resolver, physical DNS, remote shell resolver discovery without a separate security design, fake DNS, implementing transports, final profile UI, and silently changing the M1 resolver contract.","1. A TASK-ID-scoped decision references the approved M1 resolver policy and states exact degraded resolver fields, defaults, transport, endpoint or URL identity, ports, address-family behavior, timeouts, retries, and cache invalidation. 2. DNS-over-TCP through authenticated SSH is the deterministic baseline unless the approved policy explicitly selects a certificate-validated tunneled DoH alternative and its trust inputs. 3. Startup and runtime readiness rules prove degraded mode is not usable until one safe upstream is ready and that upstream failure cannot trigger physical resolver, exit-host guesswork, or unapproved vendor fallback. 4. The decision covers client UDP and TCP DNS, TC or oversized responses, DNSSEC transparency, cancellation, profile change, relay restoration, diagnostics, and zero query-name logging. 5. Downstream integration, state, leak-test, documentation, profile, and UI tasks are listed by concrete ID; any unresolved accountable resolver choice remains on the existing M1 decision instead of being guessed here.","[""TASK-260715-30lv40""]","[""TASK-260715-3260rm""]","[{""text"":""Consume the approved M1 resolver decision without inventing a second resolver policy"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Specify exact safe transport readiness failure migration and privacy behavior"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Attach the degraded DNS contract and concrete downstream impact map"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T02:04:35Z,true,[],"[{""name"":""TASK-260715-2zmw58_m1-resolver-decision.md"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-2zmw58/TASK-260715-2zmw58_m1-resolver-decision.md"",""description"":""Approved M1 resolver decision prerequisite for degraded DNS""}]",[]
TASK-260715-30lv40,task,record-capability-state-and-reason-contract,"Record the capability state, reason, and ownership contract",backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Define the binding M2 capability model and transition truth table for full, degraded, failed, and reasserting-compatible states, including readiness predicates, reason codes, generation ownership, consumer snapshots, and the boundary to M3 reconnect.","In scope: provider as live-state authority; TCP, safe-DNS, and UDP capability bits; connecting, full, degraded, failed, stopping, and relay-reprobe substates; reasserting-compatible projection; bootstrap and relay failure reasons; safe-DNS readiness; association invalidation; snapshot version and monotonic generation; app-message projection; retry eligibility; stop behavior; privacy-safe diagnostics; M1 and M3 seams. Out of scope: implementing transitions, final UI presentation, path-change reconnect, lane scheduler, includeAllNetworks fail-closed mode, analytics, and claiming absolute kill-switch behavior.","1. A TASK-ID-scoped state table defines entry, exit, required resources, allowed traffic, published capability bits, reason, cleanup, and retry behavior for every state and transition. 2. Full requires authenticated SSH, mandatory packet and TCP path, safe DNS, and a validated live relay session; degraded requires the same mandatory base without UDP; failed advertises no usable traffic. 3. Every unsupported platform, asset, bootstrap, checksum, launch, version, feature, limit, framing, health, process, lane, safe-DNS, stop, and stale-generation event maps to one stable finite reason and disposition. 4. Provider snapshots are versioned, generation-scoped, truthful under late callbacks, and expose aggregate build or limit evidence without destinations, queries, payloads, credentials, or remote-controlled strings. 5. The contract clearly assigns relay-only reprobe to M2 and path, host, route, lane-pool, sleep, NAT64, or captive reconnect to M3 with no contradictory ownership.",[],"[""TASK-260715-2zmw58"",""TASK-260715-3edgwz"",""TASK-260715-ak0s72""]","[{""text"":""Attach the state transition traffic capability and reason tables"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Prove full degraded failed and stale-generation readiness predicates"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Map exact M1 M2 M3 and UI ownership boundaries"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T02:04:35Z,false,[],"[{""name"":""TASK-260715-30lv40_m1-runtime-handoff.md"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-30lv40/TASK-260715-30lv40_m1-runtime-handoff.md"",""description"":""M1 runtime ownership prerequisite for capability states""}]","[{""name"":""TASK-260715-30lv40_capability-state-plan.puml"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-30lv40/TASK-260715-30lv40_capability-state-plan.puml"",""description"":""Planning state diagram for full, degraded, failed, and relay-only restoration""}]"
TASK-260715-3260rm,task,integrate-selected-degraded-safe-dns-transport,Integrate the selected degraded safe-DNS transport,backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Wire the approved safe-DNS upstream into degraded startup, runtime health, cache generation, and shutdown using the existing M1 DNS listener and SSH DNS-over-TCP path or the explicitly approved tunneled DoH implementation.","In scope: consume M1 DNS listener, transaction, cache, resolver, and SSH upstream components; selected transport construction; authenticated SSH dependency; readiness probe; UDP and TCP client queries; TC and length semantics; bounded connections, requests, bytes, timeouts, and retries; cache invalidation on resolver or generation change; health loss; cancellation; typed failure; metrics without query data. Out of scope: relay UDP, physical resolver fallback, new resolver selection, recursive server implementation, fake DNS, general HTTP client outside approved DoH scope, path reconnect, and final UI.","1. Degraded DNS accepts the existing virtual-network UDP and TCP clients and sends every upstream request only through the selected authenticated tunneled transport to the approved resolver. 2. Readiness is not published until transport construction and a deterministic safe health condition succeed, and runtime loss emits a typed mandatory failure before any physical fallback. 3. TCP framing or approved DoH request and certificate behavior, correlation, maximum message size, TC, timeout, cancellation, cache generation, and retry rules match the recorded policy and M1 transaction contract. 4. Connections, channels, requests, response buffers, cache entries, timers, and retries remain within fixed ceilings and return to baseline on mode change, profile change, SSH loss, and stop. 5. Controlled resolver and fake-channel tests cover both client transports, IPv4 and IPv6 resolver endpoints where approved, success, malformed response, timeout, certificate failure if applicable, cancellation, repeated cleanup, and zero physical sentinel observations.","[""TASK-260715-2zmw58""]","[""TASK-260715-uh8kk6"",""TASK-260715-kxxujt"",""TASK-260715-1vg1mb""]","[{""text"":""Integrate only the approved M1 DNS components and selected tunneled upstream"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Gate degraded readiness and fail mandatory on safe-DNS health loss"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Test protocol bounds cache generation privacy and zero physical fallback"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T02:04:35Z,true,[],"[{""name"":""TASK-260715-3260rm_m1-safe-dns-handoff.md"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-3260rm/TASK-260715-3260rm_m1-safe-dns-handoff.md"",""description"":""M1 safe-DNS component prerequisites for degraded integration""}]",[]
TASK-260715-3edgwz,task,implement-relay-capability-negotiation-and-snapshot,Implement relay capability negotiation and truthful runtime snapshots,backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Combine bootstrap results, protocol handshake, build identity, features, effective limits, relay health, and mandatory base-path readiness into one versioned generation-safe capability snapshot for the provider, containing app, diagnostics, and policy consumers.","In scope: consume the state contract; protocol and build identity; normalized platform; bootstrap reason; feature bits; effective frame and resource limits; relay generation and health; TCP and safe-DNS readiness inputs; full, degraded, or failed projection; versioned provider-message model; snapshot sequencing; aggregate counters; privacy-safe encoding; stale and future-version behavior. Out of scope: performing bootstrap, implementing M1 base paths, transition orchestration, final UI rendering, persistence as source of truth, remote-controlled diagnostic text, and path reconnect.","1. The provider publishes full only when current-generation mandatory base paths and validated relay evidence are all ready, degraded only when TCP and safe DNS are ready without UDP, and failed otherwise. 2. Snapshot schema versions capability bits, mode, finite reason, relay build identity, normalized platform, feature set, effective limits, health age, and aggregate counters with backward and future-version handling. 3. Bootstrap, handshake, health, association, and safe-DNS inputs are serialized by runtime generation and out-of-order or late events cannot regress or resurrect capability. 4. The containing app can distinguish system session state from provider capability and never infers UDP from connected or TCP from relay presence. 5. Unit and message compatibility tests cover every input combination, generation replacement, stale snapshot, oversized message, unknown reason, redaction, and deterministic no-secret serialization.","[""TASK-260715-30lv40"",""TASK-260715-159pcp"",""TASK-260715-9h7pf8"",""TASK-260715-18owh7""]","[""TASK-260715-ak0s72"",""TASK-260715-1vg1mb""]","[{""text"":""Publish versioned full degraded and failed snapshots from exact readiness predicates"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Serialize relay identity features limits health and reasons without sensitive data"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Test every input combination message version and stale-generation ordering"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T02:04:35Z,true,[],"[{""name"":""TASK-260715-3edgwz_m1-snapshot-handoff.md"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-3edgwz/TASK-260715-3edgwz_m1-snapshot-handoff.md"",""description"":""M1 versioned model and diagnostics prerequisites for M2 snapshots""}]",[]
TASK-260715-3kga9i,task,document-capability-modes-limitations-and-diagnostics,"Document capability modes, limitations, recovery, and diagnostics",backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Publish the technical and user-copy contract for full, degraded, failed, and relay-restoration behavior, including traffic tables, reason codes, DNS safety, UDP and QUIC limitations, snapshots, recovery, test evidence, privacy, and handoffs to M3 and product UX.","In scope: mode and transition diagrams; traffic matrix for TCP, DNS, general UDP, and system exceptions; readiness predicates; finite reason catalog; relay build, feature, limit, and health summaries; safe-DNS policy; UDP rejection; retry and restoration; failed behavior; provider versus system session state; diagnostics and support redaction; automated, harness, iPhone, and Mac evidence; M3 and M4 ownership. Out of scope: final visual design, marketing claims, absolute kill-switch claims, M3 path reconnect procedures, includeAllNetworks implementation, privacy policy approval, analytics, and changing behavior while documenting it.","1. Mode and transition diagrams match the implemented state contract and show exact readiness, cleanup, retry, full restoration, and failed boundaries. 2. A traffic table states what happens to new and existing TCP, UDP, DNS, and relevant QUIC-family traffic in every state and accurately discloses Apple system exceptions without a cryptographically absolute kill-switch claim. 3. The finite reason catalog maps each cause to mode, user-safe summary, operator diagnostics, retry eligibility, and remediation without destinations, queries, payloads, credentials, or remote-controlled strings. 4. Reproduction sections link state, fault, leak, harness, iPhone, and Mac evidence and list expected snapshots, captures, memory, counters, and cleanup results. 5. Concrete handoffs identify M3 lane, path, NAT64, sleep, captive, fail-closed, and QUIC work plus M4 status UI and privacy copy, and the document does not claim those later behaviors exist.","[""TASK-260715-2y78ah"",""TASK-260715-3nkhry"",""TASK-260715-10phgg""]",[],"[{""text"":""Publish mode transition traffic reason retry and diagnostic diagrams and tables"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Verify user-safe wording against automated iPhone and Mac evidence"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Link exact M3 M4 and release handoffs without claiming later behavior"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T01:58:46Z,true,[],[],[]
TASK-260715-3nkhry,task,run-iphone-full-degraded-recovery-validation,"Verify iPhone full, degraded, failed, and relay-restoration behavior",backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Run the M2 capability acceptance matrix on a named physical iPhone, proving truthful modes, TCP continuity where safe, tunnel-owned DNS fallback, explicit UDP rejection, mandatory failure on safe-DNS loss, clean relay restoration, no ordinary leak, and bounded resources.","In scope: supported physical iPhone and iOS; stable Wi-Fi and cellular starting paths; controlled authenticated SSH host; supported and unsupported relay fixture; full startup; install or handshake failure; relay process or lane loss; controlled TCP flow; general UDP probes; UDP and TCP DNS clients; safe-DNS failure; reprobe recovery; user stop; access and exit captures; snapshots; memory and counters; redacted evidence. Out of scope: Wi-Fi or cellular transition, NAT64 transition, sleep or wake, captive recovery, includeAllNetworks, final QUIC policy, TestFlight, App Review, production traffic, and system-exception claims beyond Apple behavior.","1. Evidence records device, iOS and Xcode, source and dependency revisions, profile generation, relay asset and protocol identity, reason fixture, resolver and exit fixture, network type, timestamps, duration, snapshots, memory, queues, associations, drops, and authorized captures. 2. Full startup advertises UDP only after relay validation; forced relay-unavailable startup advertises degraded only after controlled TCP and safe DNS work. 3. Runtime relay loss invalidates general UDP, keeps eligible TCP and safe DNS, reports the exact reason, and access capture shows zero ordinary UDP or DNS fallback; safe-DNS loss moves to failed. 4. Relay repair and reprobe restore full only after a fresh identity and handshake, and subsequent IPv4 or IPv6 UDP succeeds through new exit-host associations without old-generation delivery. 5. Repeated full, degraded, failed, restore, and stop rows stay within the memory budget, clean routes and resources, and attach a TASK-ID-scoped redacted reproducible evidence bundle.","[""TASK-260715-2y78ah"",""TASK-260715-2kfa02""]","[""TASK-260715-3kga9i""]","[{""text"":""Run full startup degraded startup runtime failure DNS failure restore and stop on iPhone"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Capture truthful snapshots traffic behavior and zero ordinary physical fallback"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Attach device revision memory generation cleanup and redacted evidence"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T02:04:35Z,true,[],"[{""name"":""TASK-260715-3nkhry_m1-iphone-baseline.md"",""uri"":""file://TASK-260715-3nkhry/TASK-260715-3nkhry_m1-iphone-baseline.md"",""description"":""Physical iPhone M1 acceptance prerequisite for capability validation""}]",[]
TASK-260715-ak0s72,task,implement-relay-failure-to-degraded-transition-controller,Implement relay failure-to-degraded transition control,backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,Integrate relay startup and runtime failure events into the provider coordinator so UDP associations are invalidated promptly and the tunnel enters degraded only when the existing TCP and selected safe-DNS paths remain usable.,"In scope: unsupported platform or asset; bootstrap, upload, verification, install, launch, handshake, feature, and limit failure; relay framing, health, process, or lane loss; association admission stop; client and relay state invalidation; queue and timer cleanup; safe-DNS readiness check; TCP preservation; snapshot transition; terminal failure when base path is unsafe; stop cancellation; generation safety; diagnostics. Out of scope: repairing SSH path loss, selecting a new network path, opening lane pools, M3 reasserting implementation, implementing DNS transport, QUIC policy detail, and physical route mutation beyond existing M1 safety.","1. Each declared pre-start relay failure reaches degraded without delaying usable TCP only after safe DNS is confirmed, or reaches failed when any mandatory base capability is absent. 2. Runtime relay, framing, process, or relay-lane failure atomically stops new UDP admission, invalidates all association IDs, cancels pumps, closes queues and timers, and publishes the new mode for one generation. 3. Existing and new TCP flows follow the existing M1 transport policy while degraded and no UDP datagram is rerouted to a physical or unrelated local socket. 4. Duplicate failures, simultaneous safe-DNS loss, provider stop, late relay callbacks, and replacement generation events produce one ordered cleanup and no transient false-full snapshot. 5. Fault-injection tests cover every reason, failure at every bootstrap and runtime boundary, cleanup counters, TCP preservation, safe-DNS gate, stop races, and bounded transition latency.","[""TASK-260715-30lv40"",""TASK-260715-3edgwz"",""TASK-260715-9h7pf8"",""TASK-260715-22gz6h"",""TASK-260715-3e30tx""]","[""TASK-260715-uh8kk6"",""TASK-260715-kxxujt"",""TASK-260715-1vg1mb""]","[{""text"":""Handle every startup and runtime relay reason through one generation state machine"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Invalidate UDP atomically while preserving only verified TCP and safe DNS"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Test duplicate simultaneous stop and stale-callback transition races"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T01:58:45Z,true,[],[],[]
TASK-260715-kxxujt,task,implement-bounded-relay-reprobe-and-full-mode-restoration,Implement bounded relay reprobe and full-mode restoration,backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"While the authenticated base SSH transport remains healthy, retry only the relay bootstrap and session path with bounded cancellable backoff and restore UDP capability after a completely validated new generation, without overlapping large old and new state.","In scope: eligibility from capability reason; immediate or scheduled first attempt; capped exponential backoff and jitter; stable-success reset; one in-flight probe; same authenticated transport precondition; reuse or reinstall path; fresh protocol, identity, features, and limits validation; association registry replacement; snapshot ordering; memory overlap ceiling; cancellation on stop, SSH loss, profile change, or M3 ownership event; metrics. Out of scope: resolving or reconnecting SSH host, path-interface selection, route exclusion rebuild, lane pools, retrying host-key or authentication failure, preserving UDP associations across sessions, and user-configurable infinite retry.","1. Only relay-recoverable reasons schedule reprobe and at most one attempt, timer, bootstrap state, exec channel, and prospective relay generation exist at a time. 2. Backoff is bounded and jittered from an injected source, resets after documented stable full operation, and cancels promptly on stop, base transport loss, profile change, or transition handed to M3. 3. A new session becomes full only after verified asset, process launch, protocol and identity handshake, supported features, safe effective limits, and health readiness all succeed for the current generation. 4. Old association, queue, timer, process, and channel state is released before or within the explicit overlap budget and no numeric association ID or late callback crosses generations. 5. Fake-clock and fault tests cover repeated failures, recovery at every attempt, cancellation, stale timer, base-path loss, profile change, memory pressure, stable reset, and exact snapshot sequence degraded to full.","[""TASK-260715-ak0s72"",""TASK-260715-3260rm"",""TASK-260715-9h7pf8"",""TASK-260715-19lr1c""]","[""TASK-260715-1vg1mb""]","[{""text"":""Implement one eligible bounded jittered and cancellable relay-only reprobe"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Gate full restoration on a fresh complete relay validation chain"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Test overlap budgets generations backoff reset and M3 handoff cancellation"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T01:58:45Z,true,[],[],[]
TASK-260715-uh8kk6,task,enforce-degraded-udp-rejection-and-no-fallback-policy,Enforce degraded UDP rejection and zero-fallback policy,backlog,STORY-260715-2ungml,,"Apply explicit traffic policy while degraded: preserve eligible TCP, keep DNS on the selected safe tunnel transport, reject general UDP at the private HEV adapter boundary, expose the later QUIC policy seam, and prove no packet is opened on a physical interface.","In scope: mode-gated UDP association admission; deterministic HEV or application-visible fast failure supported by the stack; close existing associations; DNS classification before general rejection; TCP capability unchanged when healthy; physical-socket factory prohibition; counters by broad reason; transition ordering; QUIC-family policy hook without final M3 behavior; provider stop and failed mode; unit and route-sentinel tests. Out of scope: synthesizing arbitrary ICMP if the stack contract does not support it, blocking system exceptions beyond Apple guarantees, final QUIC heuristics, fail-closed includeAllNetworks, path reconnect, per-application rules, analytics, and destination logging.","1. In degraded mode tunnel DNS is admitted only to the safe DNS component, eligible TCP continues through M1, and every new general UDP association receives the documented bounded rejection without relay or physical socket creation. 2. Transition from full stops new UDP admission before capability publication and existing associations are closed or failed deterministically with no datagram accepted after invalidation. 3. Failed and stopping modes admit neither TCP, DNS, nor UDP through stale runtime generations and cannot report degraded merely because the system VPN session remains connected. 4. Factory and sentinel tests prove no degraded general UDP or ordinary DNS opens a physical-interface socket or reaches a physical resolver, including timeout and fallback branches. 5. Metrics expose only aggregate admitted, rejected, closed, DNS, and reason counts and test snapshots contain no destinations, queries, payloads, or per-app identifiers.","[""TASK-260715-ak0s72"",""TASK-260715-3260rm"",""TASK-260715-1loqwb""]","[""TASK-260715-1vg1mb""]","[{""text"":""Enforce mode-gated DNS TCP and UDP behavior at the owned adapter boundaries"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Prove transition ordering and zero relay or physical socket creation when degraded"",""done"":false},{""text"":""Test failed stopping stale-generation and privacy-safe counter behavior"",""done"":false}]",,2026-07-15T01:45:00Z,2026-07-15T01:58:45Z,true,[],[],[]

codex
The prerequisite audit found the major M0–M2 work already decomposed, including the SSH engine decision, packet-plane decision, compatible routing/DNS startup, UDP relay, and capability/degraded state. I’ll reference those concrete tasks as blockers instead of reopening their scope. I’m detailing all six M3 stories now; no product/source files are being touched.
exec
/bin/zsh -lc "task-board m 'set_details(STORY-260715-2indo6, title=\"Production SSH lane pool and congestion-aware scheduler\", description=\"Build a bounded pool of two to four authenticated SSH connections to one approved host, reserve the mandatory control lane, assign each new flow to an eligible general or optional bulk lane from congestion evidence, pin the flow until close, and recover lane-local failures without leaking traffic or multiplying memory.\", scope=\"In scope: control, general, and optional bulk lane topology; shared verified identity and profile generation; lane lifecycle and admission; health, writable-window, queued-byte, RTT, channel-count, and rekey signals; control and DNS priority; best-effort bulk classification; immutable flow pinning; memory-pressure gating; lane-local recovery and degraded capability handoff; privacy-safe observability; deterministic fault tests; physical one, two, and four lane loss and head-of-line measurements. Out of scope: SSH engine selection, changing packet framing, implementing channel-window or memory-watermark algorithms owned by STORY-260715-1zzt0c, migrating live byte streams, relay wire protocol, ProxyJump, SFTP, interactive shells, or application-specific routing.\", ac=\"1. One mandatory control lane starts first and optional general or bulk lanes open only after identity, server-limit, and memory admission checks. 2. Every lane proves the same approved host identity and credential generation, and any mismatch closes that lane before channels open. 3. New flows are assigned from documented congestion and policy inputs, remain pinned until close, and Lane D never retroactively adopts an existing flow. 4. Control, relay, and latency-sensitive DNS remain serviceable under general or bulk congestion within recorded latency bounds. 5. Rekey and failed-lane states stop new assignment on the affected lane, preserve valid existing channel state where possible, and route control-lane loss through an explicit degraded or recovery transition without DNS or UDP leakage. 6. Queues, lanes, and channels stay inside configured ceilings and soft memory pressure suppresses optional capacity. 7. Deterministic tests and physical iPhone and Mac trials at one, two, and four lanes record loss, latency, throughput, queues, windows, channels, memory, CPU, errors, and cleanup and demonstrate measured head-of-line improvement without resource growth.\"); set_details(STORY-260715-1zzt0c, title=\"Channel windows, rekey, and extension memory controls\", description=\"Implement per-channel receive-window policy, automatic client and server rekey handling, a cross-layer extension memory ledger, ordered memory-pressure actions, and a hard reconnect-overlap reservation so permitted buffering and replacement transports cannot cause unbounded growth or avoidable jetsam.\", scope=\"In scope: control, ordinary, bulk, and relay window profiles; capped bandwidth-delay-product calculation; window-credit accounting; WINDOW_ADJUST withholding; byte and elapsed-time rekey; server-initiated rekey; key-exchange admission pauses; lane-local rekey failure; advisory os_proc_available_memory sampling; soft, pressure, and critical watermarks; HEV sessions and caches, socket buffers, DNS cache, relay buffers, channels, lane count, queued bytes, and reconnect overlap in one ledger; fast refusal and controlled failure; privacy-safe metrics; fault, allocation-bound, soak, and physical-memory tests. Out of scope: selecting the SSH engine, claiming advertised credit is eagerly allocated, relying on jetsam as control flow, changing relay framing, final performance constants before measurement, live flow migration, or application UI.\", ac=\"1. Initial windows are configurable per channel class, use 32 KiB and 64 KiB candidates plus a capped bulk BDP calculation, and cannot multiply an uncapped default by maximum sessions. 2. Window adjustment is bounded by the current ledger and is withheld under pressure without attempting to revoke prior credit. 3. Client byte and time thresholds and server requests survive active direct-tcpip and relay traffic, while new-channel assignment pauses safely during key exchange. 4. Rekey failure isolates the affected lane first, emits a finite reason, and cannot leak, duplicate, or migrate existing byte streams. 5. The memory controller measures all declared consumers, treats available memory as uncached advisory input, and applies the specified soft, pressure, and critical actions in order. 6. Old and replacement transports cannot coexist beyond an explicit reservation, and critical pressure releases the old transport before replacement or stops explicitly. 7. Fault and allocation tests plus a physical at-least-5-GiB mixed soak record footprint, peak, available memory, sessions, windows, queues, channels, drops, rekeys, descriptors, and cleanup with no unbounded growth or avoidable jetsam.\"); set_details(STORY-260715-2txwb7, title=\"Path-aware reconnect and route-safe reasserting state machine\", description=\"Implement a generation-safe reconnect controller that reacts to physical-path, SSH, relay, viability, and lifecycle events, reconnects through the selected physical interface, rebuilds the exact endpoint exclusion and tunnel settings, restores safe DNS and capabilities in order, and keeps ordinary traffic scoped to the VPN while reasserting.\", scope=\"In scope: explicit disconnected, connecting, connected-full, connected-degraded, reasserting, failed, and disconnecting states; NWPath and viability inputs; cached endpoint attempts; required-interface resolution and connect; actual IPv4, IPv6, or synthesized endpoint capture; verified host identity continuity; bounded jittered retry; terminal authentication and host-key behavior; generation and stale-callback rejection; old/new transport memory reservation; exact host-route exclusion replacement; atomic network settings; mandatory control lane, packet plane, TCP, safe DNS, relay, and optional-lane restoration order; provider reasserting and capability snapshots; cancellation, diagnostics, deterministic faults, and physical path-change evidence. Out of scope: silently accepting host changes, broad subnet exclusions, physical DNS fallback, preserving UDP associations across transport generations, final NAT64, sleep, or captive matrices owned by STORY-260715-3ao1u9, final route-mode implementation owned by STORY-260715-21c23x, or UI presentation.\", ac=\"1. A complete transition table defines every state, event, readiness predicate, cleanup action, retry class, capability, and published reason and rejects illegal or stale transitions. 2. Reconnect first tries verified cached endpoint IPs and uses an NWConnection constrained to the selected physical interface for fresh resolution or connect when required, then records the actual remote endpoint. 3. Host-key mismatch, authentication rejection, and invalid configuration are terminal until user or configuration change, while transient path errors use bounded jittered retry that resets only after stable service. 4. The exact endpoint exclusion and network settings are replaced for the current generation before packet reads and capability publication, with no recursive route loop or broad bypass. 5. Mandatory lane A, safe DNS, packet and TCP paths, relay capability, and optional lanes restore in the documented order, and reasserting clears only when the advertised full or degraded mode is usable. 6. User stop promptly cancels paths, timers, attempts, settings work, lanes, tasks, and descriptors, and reconnect overlap obeys the memory reservation. 7. Fault tests and physical Wi-Fi to cellular, cellular to Wi-Fi, loss, and server-address-change trials prove zero ordinary DNS or traffic fallback, bounded outage and retries, generation safety, and resource return to baseline.\"); set_details(STORY-260715-21c23x, title=\"Explicit QUIC behavior and compatible or fail-closed route modes\", description=\"Implement fast, observable destination UDP/443 policy for Auto, Allow QUIC, and Block UDP/443, plus compatible and platform-scoped fail-closed tunnel route modes that preserve exact SSH endpoint exclusions, safe DNS, accurate Apple exception semantics, and loop-free lifecycle behavior.\", scope=\"In scope: destination UDP/443 classification; explicit local unreachable or equivalent bounded failure; Auto decisions from current SSH lane loss and latency evidence; Allow forwarding through validated relay capability; policy generation and counters; unrelated UDP and DNS separation; compatible dual-stack routes; includeAllNetworks where supported; platform capability checks and documented system exceptions; exact endpoint host routes; startup, reconnect, rollback, and mode-change integration; privacy-safe diagnostics; deterministic policy and route faults; physical capture matrix. Out of scope: payload inspection, per-app routing, silent timeout-based blocking, blocking Apple traffic outside platform guarantees, absolute kill-switch claims, changing relay protocol, physical DNS fallback, fake DNS, final UX copy, or product telemetry.\", ac=\"1. Allow QUIC forwards eligible UDP/443 only through a current validated relay generation, Block UDP/443 returns the documented fast local failure, and neither mode silently times out or affects unrelated UDP or tunnel DNS. 2. Auto uses versioned lane-health inputs, deterministic thresholds and hysteresis, rejects new UDP/443 quickly when QUIC-over-SSH is predicted to underperform, and records only aggregate reasons. 3. Full, degraded, reasserting, failed, stopping, and stale-generation states each have one explicit UDP/443 outcome with no physical socket fallback. 4. Compatible mode installs dual-stack defaults and only approved exact exclusions, while fail-closed mode enables includeAllNetworks only where supported and exposes the documented system exceptions without absolute claims. 5. Startup, live mode change, endpoint replacement, reconnect, settings failure, and stop apply or roll back route and DNS settings without a recursive route, broad exclusion, false-connected snapshot, or ordinary DNS leak. 6. Unit, fault, and golden tests cover policy decisions, fast-failure latency, unrelated traffic, platform branches, exact routes, rollback, and redaction. 7. Physical iPhone and Mac captures across compatible and fail-closed modes and full, degraded, and reasserting capabilities match the disclosed traffic matrix and record OS behavior changes.\"); set_details(STORY-260715-3ao1u9, title=\"NAT64, sleep-wake, captive-network, and lifecycle hardening\", description=\"Harden and validate the M3 runtime across IPv6-only NAT64, address-family changes, sleep and wake, captive-network transitions, containing-app termination, provider stops, and repeated lifecycle loops on named physical iPhone and Mac configurations.\", scope=\"In scope: NAT64 synthesized endpoint capture and exclusion replacement; IPv4, IPv6, dual-stack, and IPv6-only transitions; sleep and wake event coalescing; path and reconnect ownership; captive negotiation within Apple system exceptions; compatible and fail-closed behavior; safe DNS continuity or explicit failure; UI process termination independence; cancellation during start; provider stop reasons; repeated start and stop; stale-event rejection; deterministic simulation; authorized physical captures; device, OS, revision, configuration, memory, route, counter, and cleanup evidence; support-matrix regression triggers. Out of scope: private Network Extension APIs, promising captive traffic is tunnelable when Apple excludes it, physical DNS fallback, automatic host-key replacement, final marketing claims, unsupported hardware, or tuning without the performance story evidence process.\", ac=\"1. A versioned device, OS, interface, address-family, route-mode, event, traffic, and capture matrix names every required row, fixture, metric, expected outcome, and evidence location before physical execution. 2. IPv6-only and NAT64 starts and transitions use the actual connected endpoint and an exact current-generation exclusion, preserve IPv4 and IPv6 application behavior where supported, and never deadlock in stale routes. 3. Sleep and wake produce at most one current reconnect sequence, cancel stale work, restore only truthful capability, and keep reconnect overlap within the memory ceiling. 4. Captive-network handling matches compatible and fail-closed disclosures and Apple exceptions while ordinary traffic and DNS remain tunneled or fail explicitly. 5. Containing-app termination does not tear down a healthy provider, while cancellation, provider stop, and repeated lifecycle loops release routes, tasks, timers, channels, associations, sockets, and descriptors. 6. Deterministic fault tests cover synthesized-address changes, sleep races, captive transitions, UI termination, stop during start, late callbacks, and API capability changes. 7. Physical iPhone and Mac evidence records every supported row, identifies red or unavailable rows without waiver, and publishes regression triggers when OS behavior differs.\"); set_details(STORY-260715-19ii11, title=\"Evidence-led packet, transport, memory, and energy tuning\", description=\"Establish a reproducible M3 baseline and tune packet, HEV, lane, window, rekey, QUIC, reconnect, memory, CPU, and energy parameters only from named physical-device evidence, preserving fixed memory ceilings, routing and DNS safety, and before-and-after regression records.\", scope=\"In scope: benchmark protocol and workload definitions; device, OS, source, dependency, profile, network-condition, and traffic metadata; physical footprint, peak, advisory available memory, CPU, energy, packet and syscall rate, throughput, time-to-first-byte, DNS latency, RTT, queues, windows, channels, HEV sessions and caches, lane count, reconnect overlap, drops, errors, and cleanup; MTU 1500, 4096, and 8500; socket buffers and effective values; adaptive batches; HEV low-memory baseline; one, two, and four lanes; 32 KiB, 64 KiB, and capped BDP windows; rekey and Auto QUIC thresholds; watermarks and retry; Instruments bottleneck analysis; before-and-after tuning; final physical resilience matrix. Out of scope: unmeasured constants, private APIs, destination or query logging, weakening correctness or leak gates for throughput, forking HEV without material evidence and regression coverage, unsupported device claims, or release marketing benchmarks.\", ac=\"1. A task-scoped protocol defines reproducible workloads, network impairments, warmup, duration, samples, statistical comparison, pass or fail rules, metadata, raw artifact references, redaction, and regression thresholds. 2. Low-overhead bounded instrumentation reconciles packet, transport, DNS, relay, lane, memory, CPU, energy, error, and cleanup counters without payloads, destinations, query names, or high-cardinality identifiers. 3. Untuned iPhone and Mac baselines cover required MTUs, lane counts, window profiles, traffic shapes, concurrency stages, loss and latency, lifecycle, and the provisional HEV configuration. 4. Each accepted parameter change has before-and-after evidence and preserves byte correctness, latency bounds, route safety, zero ordinary DNS fallback, bounded queues, and the extension memory ceiling. 5. The 25 to 30 MiB steady-state target is reported as measured engineering evidence rather than an Apple guarantee, with peak, reconnect overlap, available-memory samples, and any device variance. 6. A HEV fork is approved only if Instruments identifies bridge copies or syscall rate as a material bottleneck and a minimal upstream-trackable change demonstrates meaningful improvement with regression coverage. 7. The final named-device matrix passes lane congestion, rekey, memory pressure, path changes, NAT64, sleep and wake, captive, QUIC, and both route modes or records explicit red rows and blocking follow-up work, and publishes accepted baselines and thresholds.\" )'" in /Users/iv/Developer/relux-proxy
